---
/*
  Privacy.

  Every claim on this page is checked against the code that makes it true, and where the
  code does something less flattering than a policy would like to say, the page says the
  less flattering thing. The two that matter: video thumbnails are served by Google, so
  the browser does contact them on any page with a video on it, and the like dedupe does
  involve an address even though it never stores one.

  If a claim here stops matching the code, the code wins and this page is wrong. The
  places to check are src/lib/reader.ts for the like token and the address hash, the
  Supabase migrations for retained account data, src/lib/notifications.ts for queued
  story email, src/components/VideoEmbed.astro for the thumbnail, and
  src/layouts/Base.astro for the fonts.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';

const lastChanged = new Date(SITE.privacyUpdated).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'short',
  year: 'numeric'
});

/*
  One row per thing the site stores about a person, so somebody can find the one they
  care about without reading prose. Ordered by how little you had to do to create it.
*/
const held = [
  {
    tag: 'Cookie',
    what: 'A random token in a cookie',
    when: 'The first time you like anything',
    why: 'So the same browser cannot like one thing twice',
    gone: 'Clear your cookies and it is gone. It expires by itself after about 13 months'
  },
  {
    tag: 'Address',
    what: 'Your address, turned into something that is not your address',
    when: 'The same moment',
    why: 'A second dedupe signal, so one person with two browsers is still one like',
    gone: 'It is never stored as an address and cannot be turned back into one. Rotating the key forgets it entirely'
  },
  {
    tag: 'Auth',
    what: 'Basic profile details from the retired sign in system',
    when: 'You created an account',
    why: 'To support profiles and submissions while those features were active',
    gone: 'Email the privacy inbox and I will delete it'
  },
  {
    tag: 'Words',
    what: 'What you wrote, and when you edited it',
    when: 'You send a story, or when comments were available, a comment',
    why: 'It is the thing you asked me to publish',
    gone: 'Older comments become a tombstone. Published stories stay published'
  },
  {
    tag: 'Email',
    what: 'The email address your auth source supplied',
    when: 'You created an account',
    why: 'To finish notifications for stories already in the publication queue',
    gone: 'Email the privacy inbox and I will delete it'
  },
  {
    tag: 'Twitch',
    what: 'Your Twitch user id',
    when: 'You linked Twitch through the retired account page',
    why: 'To match you to stream history for badges, by id rather than by name',
    gone: 'Email the privacy inbox and I will delete it'
  }
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site stores about you, why, and how to get rid of it. Short, because there is not much."
>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="shell read">
      <aside class="rail">
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Who runs this</dt><dd><a href="/about/">Michael Jolley</a></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Ad trackers</dt><dd>None</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Last changed</dt><dd>{lastChanged}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <article data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-filter="kind:page">
        <header class="storyhead">
          <span class="path">privacy</span>
          <h1>
            You can read this whole site
            <span class="squiggle"><span>without telling me anything.</span></span>
          </h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Reading needs no account, no cookie banner and no consent dialog, because
            reading sets nothing that needs consenting to. Accounts and sign in have been
            retired. Everything below covers the small amount of data the site still uses,
            plus what remains from older accounts.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prose privacy-body">
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="tag">The short version</span>
            <p>
              I keep the least I can get away with and I never sell it. If an old account
              record is still here, email me and I will remove it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2>What I hold, and how to get rid of it</h2>
          <p>
            Nothing in this table exists until you do the thing in the second column.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="crows privacy-rows">
          {
            held.map((h) => (
              <div class="crow">
                <span class="eyebrow">{h.tag}</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>{h.what}</h3>
                  <p><b>Created when:</b> {h.when}</p>
                  <p><b>Why:</b> {h.why}</p>
                  <p><b>Getting rid of it:</b> {h.gone}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <div class="prose privacy-tail">
          <h2>The like button, in detail</h2>
          <p>
            Likes need no account, which means the site has to recognise a browser somehow
            or the count is meaningless. It does two things. It puts a random identifier in
            a cookie, which knows nothing about you beyond being the same browser as last
            time. And it runs your address through a keyed hash and keeps only the result,
            which is 32 characters that cannot be turned back into an address and cannot be
            compared against anybody else's records.
          </p>
          <p>
            I want to be straight about the second one rather than hide behind the word
            hashed: your address is briefly in memory on my server at the moment you press
            the button, the same as it is for every request to every website. What is
            different is that it is never written down. Change the key and the site forgets
            which browsers liked what, permanently, which is the property that makes it a
            dedupe token rather than a record of where readers live.
          </p>

          <h2>Who else your browser talks to</h2>
          <p>
            Fonts are served from this site, not from Google. There is no analytics script,
            no advertising network, no session recorder, and no tag manager. The site sets
            no cookie at all until you like something.
          </p>
          <p>
            The exception, and it is a real one: video thumbnails are served by Google from
            <code>i.ytimg.com</code>, so on any page showing a video your browser makes a
            request to Google to fetch that image, and Google sees it. The player itself is
            not loaded. A YouTube embed would pull roughly 870 KB of Google script onto the
            page and start talking to them before you had asked for anything, so instead the
            page shows a still and a play button, and clicking it takes you to YouTube,
            where their rules apply and mine stop.
          </p>
          <p>
            The pages are hosted by Netlify and the database is Supabase. Both process
            requests on my behalf, and neither gets that data to use for themselves.
          </p>

          <h2 id="retired-accounts">Retired accounts</h2>
          <p>
            The account, sign in, profile, and submission pages have been removed. If you
            had an account, <a href={`mailto:${SITE.privacyEmail}`}>email the privacy inbox</a>
            and I will delete the account record, handle, badges, auth details, and Twitch
            link.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things survive it, and you should know which before you press it. Comments
            become tombstones rather than vanishing, because deleting the middle of a
            conversation makes nonsense of the replies around it, and those replies belong
            to other people. Stories you have already had published stay published, because
            they are articles on a public site by then. If you want a published story taken
            down as well, <a href={`mailto:${SITE.privacyEmail}`}>email the privacy inbox</a>
            and I will take it down.
          </p>

          <h2>Email</h2>
          <p>
            No new account notification settings are created. A story already in the
            publication queue may still send the notification its author asked for. Every
            message has a link that turns off that type without asking you to sign in.
          </p>
          <p>
            The delivery queue stores your account id and the details needed to write the
            message, but not your email address. The address is read from your auth account
            at send time and handed to Resend, the delivery provider. When I delete the
            account data, its queued messages go with it. The site sends no welcome email,
            digest, or marketing email from that address.
          </p>
          <p>
            The .NET Drip is the one exception and it is opt in. Its signup box sits on the
            front page and a handful of others, and it is the only thing here that asks you
            to type an address. If you use it, that address goes onto the Drip list, which
						is a newsletter I send from somewhere else. It was never joined to the old
						account system, and every issue carries its own unsubscribe link.
          </p>

          <h2>Where you live</h2>
          <p>
            If you are in the UK, the EU, or somewhere with similar law, you have rights to
            see what I hold, correct it, take it elsewhere and have it deleted.
            <a href={`mailto:${SITE.privacyEmail}`}>Email the privacy inbox</a> and I will do
            it by hand.
          </p>

          <h2>Children</h2>
          <p>
            This is a site about professional software. It is not aimed at children. The
            retired account system was not intended for anyone under 16.
          </p>

          <h2>When this changes</h2>
          <p>
            The date is in the sidebar and it only moves when something actually changed.
            If a change means I hold something new, that is not a quiet edit and I will say
            so before it starts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-actions privacy-acts">
          <a class="btn primary" href={`mailto:${SITE.privacyEmail}`}>Delete old account data</a>
          <a class="btn" href="/terms/">Terms</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy-body {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .privacy-rows .crow h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .privacy-rows .crow p {
    margin: 0.2rem 0;
  }
  .privacy-tail,
  .privacy-acts {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
